<http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl> rdfs:label "OCRe study design".
<http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#OCRE100038> iao:0000115 """Phase describes the level of a trial required of drugs before (and after) they are routinely used in clinical practice: 
- Phase I trials assess toxic effects on humans (not many people participate in them, and usually without controls); 
- Phase ll trials assess therapeutic benefit (usually involving a few hundred people, usually with controls, but not always); 
- Phase III trials compare the new treatment against standard (or placebo) treatment (usually a full randomised controlled trial). At this point, a drug can be approved for community use. 
- Phase IV monitors a new treatment in the community, often to evaluate longterm safety and effectiveness. [Glossary of Terms in The Cochrane Collaboration]

A trial can be of a combination phase (e.g., I/II).
The concept of phase is not applicable to trials studying certain interventions (e.g., device, procedure, behavioral)""";
                                                       iao:0000117 "Simona";
                                                       a owl:Class;
                                                       rdfs:label "Phase";
                                                       rdfs:subClassOf pobo:BFO_0000015.
<http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#Phase_0> iao:0000115 "A Phase 0 trial is an exploratory trial involving very limited human exposure, with no therapeutic or diagnostic intent (e.g., screening study, microdose study). [http://prsinfo.clinicaltrials.gov/definitions.html]";
                                                    <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/statistics.owl#curator> "Simona";
                                                    a owl:Class;
                                                    rdfs:label "Phase 0";
                                                    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#OCRE100038>.
<http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#Phase_1> iao:0000115 "A Phase I trial assesses toxic effects on humans (not many people participate, and usually without controls) [Glossary of Terms in The Cochrane Collaboration]";
                                                    <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/statistics.owl#curator> "Simona";
                                                    a owl:Class;
                                                    rdfs:label "Phase 1";
                                                    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#OCRE100038>.
<http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#Phase_2> iao:0000115 "A Phase ll trial assesses therapeutic benefit (usually involving a few hundred people, usually with controls, but not always) [Glossary of Terms in The Cochrane Collaboration]";
                                                    <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/statistics.owl#curator> "Simona";
                                                    a owl:Class;
                                                    rdfs:label "Phase 2";
                                                    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#OCRE100038>.
<http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#Phase_3> iao:0000115 """A Phase III trial compares the new treatment against standard (or placebo) treatment (usually a full
randomised controlled trial). At this point, a drug can be approved for community use. [Glossary of Terms in The Cochrane Collaboration]""";
                                                    <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/statistics.owl#curator> "Simona";
                                                    a owl:Class;
                                                    rdfs:label "Phase 3";
                                                    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#OCRE100038>.
<http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#Phase_4> iao:0000115 "A Phase IV study monitors a new treatment in the community, often to evaluate longterm safety and effectiveness. [Glossary of Terms in The Cochrane Collaboration]";
                                                    <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/statistics.owl#curator> "Simona";
                                                    a owl:Class;
                                                    rdfs:label "Phase 4";
                                                    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#OCRE100038>.
<http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_design.owl#Single_group_study> iao:0000115 """A single group study is an interventional study that has only a single allocation group and no contemporaneuos comparison group.
A study in which an individual acts has his/her own comparison does not fall into this category, since an individual is not a group.""";
                                                               <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/statistics.owl#curator> "Simona";
                                                               <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/study_protocol.owl#develop_comment> "Simona: to be reviewed";
                                                               a owl:Class;
                                                               rdfs:label "Single Group Study";
                                                               rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/net/OCRe/research.owl#Interventional_study>.
